; Assets:Broker's `value $7` gives its lot {$5} [2020/01/01] the valuation
; (($7)), and a later posting that names the valued lot,
; `-10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01] (($7))`, takes from that same lot, so the
; Assets total is $-50 alone.
;
; Ledger adds the valuation to the lot itself, and a posting can then name
; the lot with the valuation or without it, so the later posting reaches
; the lot Assets:Broker holds.  Were the valued lot still found only by
; the annotation it was bought with, the later posting would open a second
; lot with the same annotation, which a balance would keep apart from the
; first, and the Assets total would keep 10 and -10 AAPL.

account Assets:Broker
    value $7

2020/01/01 Buy
    Assets:Broker    10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01]
    Assets:Cash     $-50

2020/01/02 Transfer out
    Assets:Other    -10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01] (($7))
    Equity:Transfers

test bal --lots Assets
                $-50  Assets
10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01] (({$7}))    Broker
                $-50    Cash
-10 AAPL {$5} [2020/01/01] (({$7}))    Other
--------------------
                $-50
end test
